    {q:"How does Volcán compare to Boquete?", a:"Very similar climate and altitude — both sit at roughly 1,200m above sea level. The key differences: Volcán is cheaper (30–50% less on rent, food, and services), has a much smaller expat community, fewer amenities and restaurants, and less tourism infrastructure. Expats who have lived in both often say Volcán offers a more authentic, less commercialized experience. The trade-off is fewer English-speaking social networks and services."},
    {q:"Is internet reliable in Volcán?", a:"Less so than in Boquete. Fiber is not widely available in Volcán, and mobile data speeds can be inconsistent. Starlink satellite internet is the recommended solution for remote workers — it provides high speeds even in the highlands and has transformed connectivity for several expats who have moved to the area. Budget around $120/month for Starlink equipment and service."},
    {q:"What makes Volcán a good value?", a:"The combination of a cool climate (no air conditioning needed, so very low electricity bills), cheap rent ($400–$600 for a house), local food prices significantly below expat hubs, and a 24/7 hospital on your doorstep. The total monthly budget for a couple can realistically come in under $1,200 — something increasingly difficult to achieve in Boquete."},
    {q:"Is there a growing expat community in Volcán?", a:"Yes, though it's much smaller than Boquete's. There are active expat residents — primarily retirees from North America — and informal social networks. The community is close-knit and welcoming. Some expats specifically prefer Volcán's smaller community because it allows for deeper integration with local Panamanian life."},
